error[L0801]: `fail` ends a function that can fail, but `no_fails` cannot fail
  --> tests/check/failures.lym:26:9
   |
26 |         fail Empty
   |         ^^^^ this would end `no_fails` with a `ParseError`
   |
help: add `fails ParseError` to the declaration of `no_fails`, after its parameters and its return type

error[L0801]: `try` passes a failure on, but `passes_without_fails` cannot fail
  --> tests/check/failures.lym:30:12
   |
30 |     return try parse(text)
   |            ^^^ on a failure this would end `passes_without_fails` with a `ParseError`
   |
help: add `fails ParseError` to the declaration of `passes_without_fails`, or handle both cases here with `match`

error[L0801]: `try` would pass on a `ParseError`, but `passes_other_failure` fails with a `string`
  --> tests/check/failures.lym:33:12
   |
33 |     return try parse(text)
   |            ^^^ a failure never changes type by itself
   |
help: fail with a `string` of your own: `try call else fail value`, or handle both cases with `match`

error[L0802]: a function cannot fail with a `list[integer64]`
  --> tests/check/failures.lym:35:57
   |
35 | function grows(integer64 count) returns integer64 fails list[integer64]
   |                                                         ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ a failure is a value that does not grow and holds no view or handle
   |
help: fail with a choice or a declared type made of numbers, booleans and text, as in `fails ParseError`

error[L0309]: a function that can fail cannot return a list or a view yet
  --> tests/check/failures.lym:38:44
   |
38 | function returns_list(integer64 _) returns list[integer64] fails string
   |                                            ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ the compiler adds this in roadmap phase 3
   |
help: let the caller pass the list with `variable` for the function to fill, and return nothing

error[L0803]: `try` needs a call to a function that can fail
  --> tests/check/failures.lym:42:16
   |
42 |     return try count
   |                ^^^^^ this is not a call
   |
help: remove `try`, or put it right before a call to a function declared with `fails`

error[L0803]: `try` needs a call to a function that can fail
  --> tests/check/failures.lym:45:16
   |
45 |     return try length("{count}")
   |                ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ `length` cannot fail
   |
help: remove `try`, or put it right before a call to a function declared with `fails`

error[L0805]: `try` with `else fail` has to be the whole value of its statement
  --> tests/check/failures.lym:48:16
   |
48 |     return 1 + try parse(text) else fail "bad"
   |                ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ this is part of a larger expression
   |
help: give it a statement of its own, as in `constant value = try read(path) else fail "cannot read"`, and use `value` here

error[L0505]: `parse` can fail, and nothing handles its result
  --> tests/check/failures.lym:51:5
   |
51 |     parse(text)
   |     ^^^^^^^^^^^ a failure here would go unnoticed
   |
help: pass the failure on with `try` and discard the value on purpose, or handle both cases with `match`
   |
51 |     constant _ = try parse(text)

